WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.220
- The longest running British med drama.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.110
We have to review it, season 36, what?

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:06.943
Let's go!

00:00:06.943 --> 00:00:07.776
Pee-woop!

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.221
(car honking)

00:00:11.221 --> 00:00:13.430
(car breaking)

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.078
Good reaction.

00:00:15.078 --> 00:00:15.911
- [Young Woman] Hey!

00:00:15.911 --> 00:00:16.996
- By both parties.

00:00:16.996 --> 00:00:18.030
- Hey, what you doing?

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:19.650
- Don't road rage, that's not healthy.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.599
- Abusing fellow road users
isn't very constructive, is it?

00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:24.458
(car engine going)

00:00:24.458 --> 00:00:25.458
(shoulder dislocating)

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:26.291
Ah!

00:00:26.291 --> 00:00:28.300
- Never grab a moving vehicle.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.330
That is an argument/fight
you will never win.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.010
And whatever sound effect I just heard

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.020
from her grabbing the car
door there, didn't sound good.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.670
Also, didn't sound super realistic.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.920
I'm assuming she's gonna have
like a dislocated shoulder

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.970
here or something, but
it didn't sound real.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.953
It sounded too meaty, it
was like (meat squishing).

00:00:44.953 --> 00:00:46.780
- He must have driven off at some speed

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:47.910
to dislocate your shoulder.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.020
- Dislocated shoulder,
the most common type

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.810
of dislocation is an anterior
shoulder dislocation.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.001
- Hey Billy, how are you feeling?

00:00:56.001 --> 00:00:58.720
- Yeah, I'm all good, I'm
ready to rock and roll.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.960
- In general, I don't like
keeping patients in slings

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.960
like this, because it actually
causes a increased rate

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.200
of frozen shoulder where
you have decreased mobility,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.110
you get less range of motion,
thereby having less healing.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.390
- Look, thank you for everything, I just,

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:12.980
I really wanna get back to my van.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.420
- Yeah so, x-rays look okay.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:16.253
- All right, well obviously,

00:01:16.253 --> 00:01:19.165
you can't drive with that shoulder.

00:01:19.165 --> 00:01:22.690
- I can't just leave it
there and, it's my home.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:24.480
- Yeah, yeah, it's your home, I get it.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.323
- It's my dad.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:27.573
He's in the back.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.230
He's got early onset dementia.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.550
You wouldn't know if you met him,

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.160
he just forgets a bit sometimes.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.800
- Even mild dementia can become
problematic when it comes

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.870
to tasks like managing your money,

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.620
paying bills, being susceptible to scams.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.190
Think about it, if you
don't have a good memory,

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.300
somebody can call and say,
you didn't pay a certain bill

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.120
asking you for personal information.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.130
You can just give it up to him.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:52.570
And speaking of scams, if you
see someone that looks like me

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.290
or using a name similar to me, giving

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.530
out WhatsApp phone numbers, that's not me.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.050
Just like the fake accounts on Instagram,

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.710
that's a fake account on
YouTube, report that bad boy.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.370
- You're gonna look for Clive, aren't you?

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:03.836
- Who's Clive?

00:02:03.836 --> 00:02:04.740
- Ugh, Lily's dad.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.980
- Look, I've just been calling the police.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.092
Let them deal with it.

00:02:08.092 --> 00:02:09.830
- The police are over stressed,
I'm saving them a job.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:11.820
- The ambulances looks
so different in the UK

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.610
than they do in the US, I've never seen

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.530
them that color, but I
guess they stand out,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.945
so, it actually makes sense
that they're this color.

00:02:18.945 --> 00:02:21.695
(dramatic music)

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:26.750
- He's not here.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.130
- Did she just leave her dad in the van?

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.915
Like I don't understand,
was he awake, was he alert?

00:02:31.915 --> 00:02:34.415
- Come on, Cardigan, waltzers.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.030
- And she said mild dementia,

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.783
like mild dementia shouldn't
interfere with navigation.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.210
- Think I might need a bit of help.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.100
- Yeah, it's quite
nasty cut you got there.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.320
Let's try and stop it bleeding, shall we?

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.753
- What are they gonna do?
- [Clive] I couldn't get up.

00:02:49.753 --> 00:02:51.180
- You got anything in there
I can use as a tourniquet?

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.940
- Yeah, you can do a makeshift tourniquet,

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.550
but those are never as good as
commercial grade tourniquets.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.520
Those can actually cut off the
circulation and you also need

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.800
to make the decision of how
bad the person's bleeding out.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.570
You can stop the bleeding
with just some pressure.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:03.560
That's a better option.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.440
Having a tourniquet on,
especially a serious one

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.350
for a long period of time
can cause harm to the limb.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.450
- Anything in there we
can use as a dressing?

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.230
- What, like a nappy, maybe?

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.510
- How about calling for help?

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:15.760
Like they're making a tourniquet, but

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.220
at the same time, they're
like, so what's up?

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:18.890
Well, you got diapers?

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:20.313
- That's the next best thing.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.310
- Did she just apply a
pad like this and hope

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.891
that it's gonna stop the bleeding
in a high pressure system?

00:03:32.891 --> 00:03:34.960
No, put some pressure on it.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.590
- [Sam] I thought it was
extremely weird, he got on a ride

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.313
at a carnival and there's no one there.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:43.970
- Look at me, ladies!

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:45.046
- No, stop it, please.

00:03:45.046 --> 00:03:47.049
Can someone help?

00:03:47.049 --> 00:03:49.092
- Okay, okay, Clive,
let's get you back in.

00:03:49.092 --> 00:03:51.327
Clive, it's okay, it's okay.

00:03:51.327 --> 00:03:54.720
Clive, Clive look at me,
look at me, it's Faith.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:55.580
Remember, from the fairground?

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.913
Now look at your arm, your arm
is bleeding, do you remember?

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.130
- That's not mild dementia anymore.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.297
If you're starting to forget where you are

00:04:03.297 --> 00:04:05.180
and you start acting this inappropriately,

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.300
that's already starting to
become moderate to severe.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.880
Also could point to
other medical problems.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:11.380
It could even be acute medical problems

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.313
like normal pressure hydrocephalus.

00:04:15.562 --> 00:04:16.830
(ominous music)

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:20.010
- Come on Ethan, let's get you inside.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.970
- No, I never want to come back here.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.880
- We need to get you
inside, get some treatment.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.033
You've got a virus.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.170
- Vitals look semi-realistic.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:33.100
We have a 91 pulse, a 96 pulse, ox.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.220
I do not see a blood
pressure on that machine.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.470
Maybe they didn't check it recently.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.000
- Ethan's got the Huntington's gene.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.770
- So Huntington's disease is a progressive

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:47.130
neurologic disorder where,
essentially the brain

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.090
and the neurologic system
starts breaking down over time.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.890
And it's a deadly, deadly illness.

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:54.690
I mean like from the time
the disease presents itself,

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.990
you have like 10 to 30 years
before death, it's that quick.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.900
And there are genetic
tests available for that.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.980
And it's even sometimes
a highly debated topic

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.470
of whether you should be tested for this.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.160
If you're not doing it
for genetic planning

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.470
for future children, finding
that out could seriously

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.510
impact your life for
the good or for the bad.

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:16.120
So it really depends on what
kind of personality you have,

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:17.620
whether or not you wanna know.

00:05:18.956 --> 00:05:21.623
(deep exhaling)

00:05:27.045 --> 00:05:28.155
(car engine roaring)

00:05:28.155 --> 00:05:29.330
- Oi, watch it!

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.776
- Sorry, turn away.

00:05:31.776 --> 00:05:33.776
- Maybe you should, uh!

00:05:36.466 --> 00:05:39.383
- Oh, that was a serious collision.

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:44.160
My thing is, if you just
crashed into someone,

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.120
have the courtesy to hit
that bad boy in reverse

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.000
and pull out so the person's not trapped.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.229
- Keep still, I know it's
scary, but try and keep still.

00:05:53.229 --> 00:05:55.453
My name's Jade, what's your name?

00:05:56.337 --> 00:05:58.280
- Emma.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:00.866
- She's really good at
keeping this person calm.

00:06:00.866 --> 00:06:01.917
- [ER Doctor] What have we got?

00:06:01.917 --> 00:06:05.590
- Hi, this is Emma Nash, 31
years old, driver involved

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:09.350
in an RTC car versus Lori
cab about 40 minutes ago.

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:11.206
She was extra cared by the firefighters.

00:06:11.206 --> 00:06:13.909
Top to toe, buggy hematoma
on the side of her head,

00:06:13.909 --> 00:06:16.910
chest wall bruising from
the seatbelt, got laceration

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:20.550
with arterial bleed on her
leg, RPC stable, GCS is 12.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.160
- This is literally not
how this should happen.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.560
This is a serious trauma,
she's bleeding out.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.640
We should have blood drawn already,

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.170
we should have tests going, she should

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.000
actually be fully
undressed because you need

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.340
to do a visual exam to
see where she's bleeding,

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.570
where the bruising's happening.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:36.630
If you've missed a laceration somewhere.

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:40.150
Because that delay in diagnosis
could cost her her life.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:41.340
- Okay, you ready to incubate?

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.510
- It should be a full team here,

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.100
like everyone's to calm and cool.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.330
This doesn't seem realistic at all.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:47.480
What is he even doing?

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.060
- Okay, let's get laryngoscope,

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.333
ET tube and Ambu bag on standby, please.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.670
- Okay, those are the
right tools for intubation.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.760
I loved that, it was him opening her jaw,

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:02.079
but she actually went,
and opened it for him.

00:07:02.079 --> 00:07:03.390
- What happened here?

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:04.990
- [ER Doctor] Sorry, you can't be in here.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:07.360
- Emma, Emma, who did this, what happened?

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:08.650
- Can I get you to wait outside, please?

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:10.330
- This is why he needs more staff,

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:11.990
so he's not doing this alone.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:14.210
This is so weird for me to watch.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:17.330
There's literally usually
seven more people in the room.

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:19.600
Drawing labs, controlling
the patient's family,

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.660
doing chest compressions
if need be, putting in IVs,

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.490
gathering the patient's belongings,

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:27.010
entering data into the
system, putting more orders.

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:28.210
Like there's so many things that need

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:29.580
to happen all at the same time.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:30.860
- You can't be in here right now.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:32.000
- She's my sister!

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.050
- Also, you need to make sure that

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:34.499
you've intubated correctly and it's not

00:07:34.499 --> 00:07:35.332
in the patient's stomach.

00:07:35.332 --> 00:07:36.165
Inside the patient's stomach.

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:39.630
- I'm a doctor, just
tell me what happened.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.063
- Okay, I'll explain everything outside.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:49.300
- Oh my God, did he intubate
it into her stomach?

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:50.133
No!

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:52.360
- She's been seriously injured.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:55.410
I need to speak to the
neurosurgeons and they'll,

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:58.380
they'll decide whether
she'll need surgery.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:07:59.770
- I wanna stay with her.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.330
- Can I get you to wait in reception?

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:04.200
I promise I'll look after her.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:05.980
- This is such an awkward conversation.

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:08.280
Nothing about this seems
genuine or authentic.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.780
- Uh, hello, yes, it's Ethan
Hardy calling from the ED.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:14.830
- He's calling for a neurosurgery consult,

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:17.570
which like, okay, fine, you
can call that, place the order,

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:18.940
whatever, call who you need to call it.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.760
But like the fact that
he's like not taking care

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.461
of the patient right now is a big problem.

00:08:22.461 --> 00:08:24.293
- How long has she'd been like this?

00:08:24.293 --> 00:08:26.930
Didn't you check the placement
of the tubes in esophagus?

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:30.220
- Oh no, that's why you need
more people in the room.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:32.290
Who is the one bagging the patient?

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:34.170
Remember he didn't hook
her up to a ventilator,

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:35.890
he hooked her up to a bag, so someone had

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:37.390
to be sitting there, squeezing it

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:39.400
and watching her pulse go up

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.470
and her oxygen saturation go down.

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:43.030
- You're here for Emma
Nash, is that right?

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:44.314
You're her sister?

00:08:44.314 --> 00:08:45.147
- I am.

00:08:45.147 --> 00:08:47.120
- If this is about Emma, I'm her fiancee.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:48.846
- She suffered a significant head injury

00:08:48.846 --> 00:08:50.450
and she may need neurosurgery

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:52.730
once the surgeons have assessed her.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:56.000
Her brain was starved of
oxygen for short while.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.790
And we won't know yet if there's
been any permanent damage.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:00.730
- What does that mean, how
did she end up like that?

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.630
I thought you said this
was a car accident.

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:04.210
- It's too early to tell.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:05.860
And if the neurosurgeons
can't offer an operation,

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:06.840
we'll need to stabilize her.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.340
- You didn't say any of
this before, what changed?

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:11.423
- No.

00:09:11.423 --> 00:09:13.520
- You intubated, patients
don't just lose oxygen.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:15.530
Something else happened,
something he's not telling me.

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:16.860
- That's so not true.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.930
Patients absolutely do, in the process

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.260
of intubation go a short
time without oxygen.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.780
It's not like a patient stops breathing

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.210
and you right away are now inserted

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:25.360
into the patient's airway.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:26.580
The fact that this doctor knows

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.520
that something's wrong already is weird.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.140
Obviously they're being sketch.

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:33.280
That's part of it, but, just
a whole weird situation.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:36.000
- I intubated her esophagus by mistake.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.030
- [Man] But you recovered
it straight away,

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:38.130
you've tracked the O2 trace on it.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.560
- Yeah, yeah, I've done that.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:40.660
- I could've killed her.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.560
- [Man] It's a recognized
complication of an intubation.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.330
- It's true, when you're
intubating a patient,

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.720
the risk is that you intubate
their esophagus by accident.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.730
That's why you check, you
can check the O2 sensor

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:51.610
or you could put the little
stethoscope on the lovely chest

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.080
and see if the chest is
rising, not the stomach.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:55.380
But another thing you can do is obviously

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.570
get a portable chest x-ray for placement,

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:00.070
but, that is obviously not
ideal because it takes longer.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:01.804
- [Stevie] Gonna introduce me?

00:10:01.804 --> 00:10:05.250
- Of course, sorry, Ethan now
our new consultant, Dr. Nash.

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:08.443
- Stevie, please, and I know who you are.

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:13.163
You're Dr. Hardy, right?

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:15.723
- Yes, I am.

00:10:15.723 --> 00:10:18.170
- Well that's her daughter
or sister or whatever.

00:10:20.583 --> 00:10:22.890
(ominous music)

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:24.240
Is she gonna do something bad?

